#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "27249c57-7f17-5e3e-9a21-0518320f5076" :question/math-academy-id "e-8192" :question/problem "A triangular plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the lines $x=1,y=0$ and $y=x$, as shown above. The plate's mass density function $\\lambda$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = xy^2$. Find the total mass of the triangular plate.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2025/Source/Images/f15db6d8baa8892b061cfaa81871fd0e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region $D$ with mass density\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = x^2y\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)xy^{2}dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^1 \\int_{0}^{x} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∫_{0}^{1}x[∫_{0}^{x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "d565cc1a-f78f-5712-8a03-df4d4e8f3e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-8193" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{(x,y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$." :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nwith charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &= ∬_(D)(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A = \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}[∫_{0}^{1}(4 - 3y^{2} + 2x)dx]dy &= ∫_{0}^{2}[4x - 3y^{2}x + x^{2}]_{0}^{1}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(4(1) - 3y^{2}(1) + (1)^{2}) - (4(0) - 3y^{2}(0) - (0)^{2})]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}(5 - 3y^{2})dy &= [5y - y^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (5(2) - (2)^{3}) - (5(0) - (0)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (10 - 8) - 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128964 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "92b9b5ea-1153-580a-a03a-94338f30fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8195" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density $\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2025/Source/Images/c3d92d5e0c81c1871fad053bd3726a50.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,0\\right)\n$$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density\n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{x = r\\cos θ; y = r\\sin θ\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=9\n$$\n\nin polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) &= 9 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nr &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}dA &= ∬_(Δ)\\frac{4r\\cos θ}{\\sqrt{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ}}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}∫_{0}^{3}4r\\cos θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[∫_{0}^{3}rdr]dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{3}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π}^{3π/2}4\\cos θ(\\frac{9}{2} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= 18∫_{π}^{3π/2}\\cos θdθ \\\\\n&= 18[\\sin θ]_{π}^{3π/2} \\\\\n&= 18[- 1 - 0] \\\\\n&=-18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q =-18$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0dc441-0236-59a8-a8a1-c86f956c5cd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127239} {:answer/id #uuid "dccd5c27-287a-576e-9a47-c0a8fd419eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1bb420-5802-559c-bdf0-4d3553e0d996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127241} {:answer/id #uuid "5066a334-b266-538e-8fca-44a2257cc4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127242} {:answer/id #uuid "600f232c-cbaf-5c0c-af1c-b505c33f84fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "600f232c-cbaf-5c0c-af1c-b505c33f84fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127243} :answer-field/id #uuid "6baaa5a7-39ce-5f3a-b3a9-673aa04335e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127238}] :question/id #uuid "948a84fa-2589-57e2-8b27-de170c3c28f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38248" :question/problem "A metal plate occupies the region $D = {(x, y): - 1 \\le y \\le 1, 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}}$. The charge density of the plate is given by $ρ(x, y) = xy^{2}$. Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate."}]}
